the first signs of defiance since the  taliban took power in afghanistan are  emerging demonstrations have been  reported in several cities witnesses say  the taliban shot dead three protesters  in jalalabad who were waving the  national flag for the latest we can  speak now to journalists franz marty who  is standing by in kabul france we saw  images of protests yesterday in several  cities and violence what is the  situation today  the protests were apparently like quell  yesterday especially in jalalabad where  you have seen the gunfire i also talked  to people in hosts  where a similar protest has taken place  yesterday  there one source said two people were  killed  after hoisting the national flag  that said the taliban yesterday taliban  members have on social media now comes  that there will be a complete curfew in  horse  this seems to be at least partly  implemented now today in the morning uh  in the same tweets that taliban  announced also clearing operation host  apparently uh triggered by this protest  um to what extent this has actually  happened is hard to say two sources in  hosts said uh they either heard no  gunshots or only few  um they mentioned that the taliban did  patrols uh but i haven't seen any signs  uh of of a vast  clearing operation in reaction uh to  that protest  france i want to ask you about the  people who are still trying to leave the  country because we've seen reports that  the taliban are still blocking people  from reaching evacuation flights that  they are beating people away from the  airport and the u.s and allies are  trying to secure safe passage for these  people so what is the situation at the  airport  the situation at the airport is chaotic  there's also conflicting information so  i was told uh today in the morning the  gates to the airport are closed  um  this seems to uh have been a result of  both the  american side and uh the taliban side uh  being afraid that uh things get even  more out of control uh however there  have been people like reaching the gate  today so it's not a complete blocking of  the routes to the airport as far as i  can say  but for example i'm in direct contact  with an african-american citizen who was  told to come today in the morning  arrived and had no chance to get in as  the gates were apparently close and then  returned um so it is still chaotic uh  even people that are told to come can't  get in  what about the polit political situation  france we've been talking about the fact  that the taliban are saying they are not  the taliban of old and we did see senior  taliban figures meeting the ex-president  hamid karzai how inclusive could their  government be  this is remains to be seen however i  don't think chances are good for an  inclusive government  if one reads the taliban statements that  they have published  since last year were especially listens  to a speech that a high-ranking taliban  member gave briefly after the signing of  the u.s taliban agreement  there he said explicitly to government  officials lower ranking government  officials who repent  who acknowledge that what they did  during the past year was a mistake and  apologize for this  they are allowed to work in the  government under the taliban that's  apparently their idea of inclusiveness  so that they would really share power  significantly share power with other  factions is hard to see and frankly  speaking the way that they now took over  the country why should they um they see  themselves as victors um so they don't  need to share power with everyone  and all uh this talk about inclusivity  is probably at least in my assessment uh  will be rather superficial than an  actual significant power sharing  journalist france marty speaking to us  from kabul thank you  president joe biden has said the u.s  military might need to stay in kabul  past the august 31st withdrawal deadline  in an abc news interview biden rejected  criticism of the pull-out and said every  last american would be evacuated  while germany's government is also  evacuating people from afghanistan after  a slow start evacuations have picked up  speed around 900 people have now been  airlifted from kabul german military  aircraft are moving back and forth now  between kabul and the uzbek capital  tashkent to bring people out and from  there evacuees are being flown to  germany on commercial flights  and our reporter benjamin alvarez-gruber  is at frankfurt airport he's been  following the arrival of evacuees for us  benjamin can you tell us what things are  like where you are  good morning sumi the situation is now  calm here at the airport there are still  some family members and friends of the  evacuees waiting for them here when they  arrived in the early morning it took  quite a time because behind me you can  see the terminal where people and  families are coming out so it's a pretty  long process first we have the border  control by police then they have a covet  19 test and then some of them the afghan  refugees are handed over to the federal  ministry of migration and refugees  we arrived in the early morning the  plane landed from uzbekistan before 4 am  so pretty early similar to the plane  that arrived here yesterday more than  900 have been evacuated from kabul and  they will continue as well we spoke to a  woman who told us that her father 61  year old father had to wait for more  than 20 hours outside of the airport in  kabul to even make it inside so the  situation is still pretty dramatic for  them and we were also able  to talk to some of the people some of  the evacuees and who were on this flight  and this is what they told us  the world can't just leave and desert  the people of afghanistan  they're in a very bad situation and the  people need help  there is nothing cheaper than life  itself in afghanistan right now  the world is playing a horrible game  with us  the taliban have arrived in kabul a few  people have taken over it's a joke a  joke  harrowing testimonials that have one  thing in common on one side the relief  they made it out of afghanistan but on  the other side the uncertainty when it  comes to those who had to be left behind  all right benjamin alvarez gruber  reporting for us at frankfurt airport  this morning thank you very much  the u.n refugee agency has described the  human toll of hostilities in afghanistan  as immense it says that half of  afghanistan's population more than 18  million people are in need of  humanitarian assistance  conflict the coronavirus pandemic and  drought are behind the disaster issues  that have led to the displacement of  millions of afghans there have been  urgent calls by international  organizations and ngos to act decisively  to help those in need  well the crisis in afghanistan is  particularly hard on children amid the  chaos and violence scores of young  afghans are among the injured and killed  an estimated 75 thousand children have  had to flee their homes in the past  month and unicef says that millions of  minors suffer from malnutrition more  than half of them are under the age of  five  many of those children are at risk of  long-term health problems as a result  the number of children in need of  humanitarian aid is some 10 million  that's almost a quarter of afghanistan's  population  let's get some more perspective on the  story now we can speak to naheed  shahalimi she's an afghan-canadian human  rights activist filmmaker and artist and  she's based in munich here in germany  thank you very much for joining us you  spent the first years of your life and  your childhood in afghanistan tell us  first of all what goes through your mind  when you see the images that we see  coming from afghanistan  um thank you for having me here i don't  watch um this is actually the first time  that i watched in in two days because  we haven't slept in five days  um we're on the phone with our staff  because i have a lot of projects in  afghanistan that are running and there's  another one that's supposed to start in  about four weeks  um the the focus right now for me is a  solution oriented focus which means get  the people that we have fought for  the past 20 years to emancipate them to  empower them to democratize them um  you know the funding was was given by  the same by the same international  community that basically left them alone  there we have the moral obligation to  get these people out that is my focus at  this moment so um we have we're building  with um especially with the with the  tech community um uh i have just i'm  launching actually today in a few hours  the press release is gonna leave and  it's a campaign that is for solidarity  of afghanistan to have concrete  strategies on how we do it in which  phases as far as the taliban are  concerned we can everything that we're  talking about is  basic based on assumptions until we know  what kind of this inclusive um a  government that they're talking about  until we know the frameworks of which we  are allowed to work  we will never be able to assess or or or  even project anything or it's all  assumptions so for this moment this  campaign for me is extremely important  and the standard for unity campaign  existed before and now this part of it  is with nfts and the nfts in the tech  world that is standing by us through  creativity  we get these funds in cryptocurrency and  the decentralization part is extremely  important because of the fact that banks  are closed and all the assets of these  displaced people right are either frozen  that's that's what's gonna happen so  that's what i'm focusing on at this  moment  well you are helping people on the  ground you have many contacts there with  the projects as you said that you're uh  working on at the moment what are people  on the ground particularly afghan women  telling you about what they're  experiencing  um those that we are in touch with are  of course there's a lot of fear  uncertainty um everything that we are  are seeing this this this great behavior  that um taliban are showing uh is slowly  changing in action so as  the fear is completely there everybody  is afraid um a lot of them are in hiding  we are trying to make sure that that we  don't reveal the sources um and keep  them where they are so that we don't we  don't post and all of those things that  people do i just ask people  not to not to bring out posts that may  jeopardize the lives of these these  women especially these activists the  journalists the um the athletes the  artists all of these people that we are  working with a lot of their lives will  be engaged in danger so please do not  reveal  things that you're not supposed to  reveal just because you want to make a  post the best way is to put pressure on  the governments to take as many out as  possible especially those that are in um  you know in in danger of being  persecuted and they will be because they  have started we have reports that people  are looking for people we have reports  that that there are certain activists  that are being uh you know  after so these are these are real  reports  naheed could you ever see yourself  returning to afghanistan  yes  i will i will i will i do see myself  returning my  returning but with the help of the  international community so that we can  do those um those projects especially  the one that's coming up i i  conceptualized that for six years i've  been i've been working on this and it's  gotten through and the idea is to put it  in a framework which would be acceptable  for the taliban and it's an all-female  concept and and i'm hoping they i mean  we have been okayed  and it's supposed to go on i'm not gonna  reveal the name of the institution or am  i gonna say where it is however i do  hope that i i'd be able to go there in  the next couple of months because we  were supposed to go in 10 days  so  yes i do i do them absolutely because we  need we need to work with them  we'll have to leave it there  afghan canadian human rights activist  filmmaker artist thank you very much for  joining us here on dw

